1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is a reproducibility study of Graph Edit Networks,</font>

2
00:00:04,013 --> 00:00:11,407
<font color="#FFFFFF">a graph neural network output layer that predicts
how a graph changes over time as a sequence of interpretable edits.</font>

3
00:00:11,487 --> 00:00:17,220
<font color="#FFFFFF">The reproduction re-implements the model and
re-tests the paper's four experimental claims.</font>

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:21,655
<font color="#000000">Graph time-series prediction asks a model to
forecast the next graph in a sequence,</font>

5
00:00:21,735 --> 00:00:25,790
<font color="#000000">not just a label.</font>

6
00:00:25,870 --> 00:00:29,925
<font color="#000000">Standard graph networks emit node or edge probabilities,</font>

7
00:00:30,005 --> 00:00:34,060
<font color="#000000">which can't naturally express the structural
operations that transform one graph into the next.</font>

8
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:38,430
<font color="#000000">Graph Edit Networks close this gap by predicting an
explicit,</font>

9
00:00:38,510 --> 00:00:40,232
<font color="#000000">human-readable edit script.</font>

10
00:00:40,312 --> 00:00:45,836
<font color="#000000">The original work claims it beats every baseline,
reaches perfect accuracy on trees,</font>

11
00:00:45,916 --> 00:00:51,308
<font color="#000000">and scales to large graphs, but those claims rest
on briefly-described benchmarks,</font>

12
00:00:51,388 --> 00:00:54,110
<font color="#000000">where independent reproduction adds value.</font>

13
00:00:54,490 --> 00:01:00,339
<font color="#000000">The reproduction contributes four things: it
re-runs the model and baseline to check each claim;</font>

14
00:01:00,419 --> 00:01:04,107
<font color="#000000">it documents the synthetic data generators the
paper omitted;</font>

15
00:01:04,187 --> 00:01:07,813
<font color="#000000">it adds a cleaner setup separating training and
test series;</font>

16
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:12,630
<font color="#000000">and it shows some benchmarks let the model win by
memorising seen transitions.</font>

17
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:19,455
<font color="#FFFFFF">A graph edit network attaches to a standard GNN
backbone and predicts a script of edits: insert,</font>

18
00:01:19,535 --> 00:01:23,126
<font color="#FFFFFF">delete or replace a node, or insert or delete an edge.</font>

19
00:01:23,206 --> 00:01:27,272
<font color="#FFFFFF">Applying them in sequence maps the current graph to
the next.</font>

20
00:01:27,352 --> 00:01:32,166
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training uses reference mappings from
graph-edit-distance approximators,</font>

21
00:01:32,246 --> 00:01:35,429
<font color="#FFFFFF">with two loss variants, hinge and cross-entropy,</font>

22
00:01:35,509 --> 00:01:38,080
<font color="#FFFFFF">and edge-filtering for citation graphs.</font>

23
00:01:38,460 --> 00:01:43,160
<font color="#000000">Benchmarks span three families: dynamical graph
systems like Edit Cycles,</font>

24
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,125
<font color="#000000">Degree Rules and Game of Life;</font>

25
00:01:45,205 --> 00:01:50,691
<font color="#000000">tree systems for Boolean-formula simplification and
Peano addition; and, for scaling,</font>

26
00:01:50,771 --> 00:01:56,453
<font color="#000000">an arXiv citation network of about twenty-seven
thousand papers yielding fifteen hundred</font>

27
00:01:56,533 --> 00:01:59,400
<font color="#000000">sub-graphs up to nearly three thousand nodes.</font>

28
00:01:59,780 --> 00:02:07,324
<font color="#000000">Three of the four original claims hold: the model
beats the variational-autoencoder baseline on every dynamical task,</font>

29
00:02:07,404 --> 00:02:12,862
<font color="#000000">reaches near-perfect accuracy on trees, and its
forward pass grows sub-quadratically.</font>

30
00:02:12,942 --> 00:02:17,098
<font color="#000000">The fourth fails: backward passes were claimed to
scale linearly,</font>

31
00:02:17,178 --> 00:02:20,030
<font color="#000000">but the fitted exponent is clearly above one.</font>

32
00:02:20,410 --> 00:02:26,717
<font color="#000000">Two robustness checks: swapping the ad-hoc
initialisation for Erdős–Rényi and configuration-model</font>

33
00:02:26,797 --> 00:02:29,153
<font color="#000000">generators barely changes any metric,</font>

34
00:02:29,233 --> 00:02:33,170
<font color="#000000">and adding a proper held-out test set lowers scores
slightly.</font>

35
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:38,833
<font color="#000000">But a deeper diagnostic finds the tree generators
mostly produce unsimplifiable trees,</font>

36
00:02:38,913 --> 00:02:44,364
<font color="#000000">only thirteen percent of Boolean and twenty-six
percent of Peano samples are usable,</font>

37
00:02:44,444 --> 00:02:46,010
<font color="#000000">undercutting those tasks.</font>

38
00:02:46,390 --> 00:02:50,466
<font color="#FFFFFF">The verdict is nuanced: Graph Edit Networks are
reproducible,</font>

39
00:02:50,546 --> 00:02:53,736
<font color="#FFFFFF">elegant and interpretable, and most claims hold.</font>

40
00:02:53,816 --> 00:02:58,164
<font color="#FFFFFF">But one scaling claim is wrong, backward passes are
super-linear,</font>

41
00:02:58,244 --> 00:03:04,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">and several benchmarks reward memorising seen
transitions rather than genuine generalisation.</font>
